On Thu, 1 Mar 2007, Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
>
> A brief side issue.
>
> One of the things that you want to learn how to do
> before mucking about with the source code is
> wrapping your C code changes in C precompiler code
> so that it is easy to turn your changes off or on at
> compile time; for example (ignoring the real code)
> if the call were
>
> heal(HowManyPoints,WhatMaxHpBoost);
>
> then you'd want to make that compilation conditional [...]

> heal(HowManyPoints,
> #ifdef NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_BOOSTS
> 0
> #else
> WhatMaxHpBoost
> #endif
> );

   Bleah! I personally don't think that /every/ patch needs this 
conditional stuff, since, after all, if some random user decides he 
doesn't want to use your patch, all he has to do is /not install it/!
So most of my patches don't bother with the #ifdef stuff.
   But if you do use #ifdef, please *PLEASE* write

     #ifdef NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_BOOSTS
             heal(HowManyPoints, 0);
     #else
             heal(HowManyPoints, WhatMaxHpBoost);
     #endif

instead of what KPD suggested! Not only is it shorter and more 
human-readable, but it's more computer-readable too; you might
find that the "patch" program will be happier dealing with
overlapping patches if they preserve the original lines' text in
this way, rather than splitting up one original source line across
seven new lines.

> You'll hate yourself later if you don't have this
> figured out and employed from the first change you
> make, trust me. Besides making the compilation
> conditional on the defined constant
> NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_BOOSTS, that constant also serves
> as a marker showing everywhere in the source code
> your change applies, making it easy to find later.

   grep 'diff -' < mypatch343.diff
works just as well, if you still have the original patch file
around. :)  YMMV.

